HOW TO BUILD COPTER COMMANDER

________________________________________________________________

Short form:


  cd copter-commander-VERSION
  emacs Makefile                # adjust variables if needed
  make                          # check for errors
  make install

then try the command "copter-commander"

________________________________________________________________

Long form:

You REALLY want gnome-libs-1.2.8-0_helix_1 or thereabouts installed on
your system.  That's what I've been testing against.  If you have no
gnome, you can make due with glx-copter-commander, see "Making due with
glx-copter-commander", below.

You also need the gtk-gl-area library installed.  See
http://www.student.oulu.fi/~jlof/gtkglarea

If you're getting poor performance, visual artifacts, laggy mouse, etc,
check out http://www.speakeasy.org/~morse/copter-commander/opengl.html

Sound is supported, sort of.  If you have OpenAL (www.openal.org) on your
system, then set the COCO_SOUND entry in the makefile to openal.  If you
have gnome on your system, but no OpenAL, set it to esd.  Otherwise set it
to mute.  The problem with both OpenAL and ESD is that they take too much
CPU, and this results in lost frames of animation.  For that reason a C-s
hotkey was introduced, to turn it on and off when you need too.  Also, I
know that the sound is played as a seperate thread, so a MP machine should
have good performance.  But, dammit, the sound card has specialized
hardware for doing this kind of stuff, it shouldn't tie up the cpu!  If
anyone has advice on this, please tell.  How do other games do it?

________________________________________________________________

Making due with glx-copter-commander:

Two executables are made: copter-commander, linked against gnome
libraries, and glx-copter-commander, linked against bare X and OpenGL.
This version has an obvuscated UI, but its easy to build on wierd systems
without gnome, say, Irix.  

Most of your setup you do with the command line.  First start a server:

  glx-copter-commander server --level balloon-run

Then start a client with

  glx-copter-commander --host <SERVER>

The client and host may run on the same machine without much slowdown.

Once the client has started, put your mouse over the window and press 'p',
then space.  (Or maybe the other way round?).  The first negotiates a
connection.  The second starts the game, if it hasn't already.  Yes,
there's NO visual feedback for the connection being made, or players
joining.  That's why this is deprecated.  :|

________________________________________________________________

Troubleshooting:

Contact copter-commander-devel@bomberlan.net
